Digoxin Accumulation Experiments—Digoxin accumulation in the various cell lines was measured by seeding \(1\times10^{6}\) cells in a single well of a six-well plate, followed by incubation of the cells for 3 days. The medium was replaced by \(1.5\ \mathrm{ml}\) of medium containing \(10\ \mu\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{[^{3}H]}\) digoxin \((0.25\ \mu\mathrm{Ci/ml})\). After a \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) incubation period, the medium was removed, and the cells were washed twice with \(1\ \mathrm{ml}\) of ice-cold phosphate-buffered saline. The cells were scraped and removed from the wells in \(0.5\ \mathrm{ml}\) of phosphate-buffered saline. Radioactivity was determined by liquid scintillation counting. Accumulation was measured in triplicate in the presence and absence of \(5\ \mu\mathrm{M}\) PSC 833.

Drug Transport Assays—Drug transport assays were performed as described (25, 29) with minor modifications. Cells were seeded on microporous polycarbonate membrane filters (pore size \(3.0\ \mu\mathrm{m}\), diameter \(24.5\ \mathrm{mm}\), TranswellTM, CostarR) at a density of \(1.5–2\times10^{6}\) cells/filter. The cells were grown in complete medium for 3 days with a medium replacement the day after seeding. One hour before the start of the experiment, medium at both sides of the monolayer was replaced. The experiment was started by replacing the medium at either the apical or the basolateral side by medium containing \(\mathrm{^{3}H}\)-labeled substrate drugs \((0.25\ \mu\mathrm{Ci/ml})\) and inulin \(\mathrm{[^{14}C]}\) carboxylic acid \((0.025\ \mu\mathrm{Ci/ml}, 4.2\ \mu\mathrm{M})\). The cells were incubated at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\), and \(50\ \mu\mathrm{l}\) aliquots were taken from both compartments at \(t=1\), 2, 3, and \(4\ \mathrm{h}\). The appearance of radioactive drug in the opposite compartment was measured and presented as the fraction of total radioactivity added at the beginning of the experiment. Directional transport was measured in duplicate. The paracellular flow was monitored by the appearance of \(\mathrm{[^{14}C]}\) inulin in the opposite compartment and was always less than \(1.5\%\) of total radioactivity per hour.

Inhibition of transport was measured similarly. One hour before the start of the experiment, the medium at both sides of the monolayer was replaced by complete medium containing the appropriate concentration of reversal agent. At \(t=0\), medium in one of the compartments was replaced by medium with radioactive drug and the indicated amount of reversal agent.

Lipid Transport Assays—Transport of \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-PC and \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-glucosylceramide (\(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-GlcDer) was measured as described (23, 30). LLC-PK1-derived cell lines were seeded on microporous membrane filters and grown for 4 days. Lipid precursors, \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-PA or \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-ceramide, were complexed to \(1\%\) (w/v) bovine serum albumin (BSA) in HEPES-buffered Hanks' balanced salt solution \((\mathrm{pH}\ 7.35)\) (HBSS) to the following concentrations: \(25\ \mu\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-PA and \(5\ \mu\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-ceramide. To discriminate surface synthesized \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-PC (30) from transport of intracellularly synthesized \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-PC, cells were preincubated

with \(10\ \mu\mathrm{Ci}\) of \(\mathrm{[^{3}H]}\) choline/ml HBSS for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\) at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), after a \(1\ \mathrm{h}\) choline depletion in HBSS at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Incubation with the lipid precursors in HBSS plus BSA followed for \(3\ \mathrm{h}\) at \(15\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the presence of \(10\ \mu\mathrm{Ci}\) of \(\mathrm{[^{3}H]}\) choline. For inhibition studies, the appropriate concentration of reversal agents was present \(1\ \mathrm{h}\) before and during the \(3\ \mathrm{h}\) incubation at \(15\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). During the incubations, short-chain lipid products appearing on the cell surface were depleted into the medium by the BSA. After \(3\ \mathrm{h}\), the apical and basolateral medium were collected, and the cells were washed in HBSS plus BSA for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) on ice. The apical and basolateral washes were pooled with the respective incubation media, the filters were cut from the wells, and the lipids from media and cells were analyzed.

Lipid Analysis—Lipids were extracted from the cells and media by a modified Bligh and Dyer extraction (31). The upper phase contained 20 mM acetic acid. The organic (lower) phase was dried under \(\mathrm{N_{2}}\), and the lipids were applied to TLC plates. Lipid products were separated in two dimensions by borate-TLC as described (32) but are \(20\ \mathrm{cm}\) in the first (alkaline) dimension. In the case of \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-\(\mathrm{[^{3}H]}\) PC in cellular lipid extracts, the fluorescent spot was circled, and the plate was exposed to a film to allow accurate separation of \(\mathrm{C_{6}}\)-NBD-\(\mathrm{[^{3}H]}\) PC and long-chain \(\mathrm{[^{3}H]}\) PC. Fluorescent spots were quantitatively analyzed in a fluorimeter, radiolabeled spots were detected by fluorography, and the radioactivity was quantified by liquid scintillation counting as described (33).

